- Used by the height graph: no

## Feature summary (web, SideFX reference)

- Source: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/pyro_advect.html
- Summary (EN): Pyro Advect moves the contents of VDB volumes along a velocity VDB, the core step for transporting smoke or fire in a Pyro simulation. It can also advect the velocity field itself, or skip that step when another Pyro Advect already did it.
- Résumé (FR) : Le nœud Pyro Advect transporte les volumes VDB le long d'un champ de vitesse, étape centrale du transport de fumée ou de feu ; la page ne couvre pas la version 2.0.

- Inputs meaning: ["data: cable of all the VDBs to advect", "v: vector VDB driving the advection, which can optionally be self-advected"]

- Key parameters: ["Scale: global multiplier on velocity values", "Ambient Velocity: constant global velocity added to the advection, usable as a wind effect", "Scale by Timestep: scales velocity by the timestep", "Trace Method: integration scheme per trace step, from Forward Euler (fastest, most diffusion) to Runge-Kutta 4 (most accurate, most expensive)", "Path Tracing: several trace steps to follow curved paths, preserving fine detail and vortices", "Maximum Steps: cap on path-tracing steps", "CFL Condition: approximate voxels stepped per trace step; lower is more stable but slower", "Adaptive Step Size: adjusts the step size dynamically", "Enable Data Advection / Scheme: BFECC, Euler + Sharpen or Euler; BFECC preserves sharpness best but costs the most", "Sharpen: counters diffusion; values that are too high cause grid artifacts", "Enable Velocity Advection / Method: self-advection of the velocity VDB and its scheme; can be turned off if another node already advected it", "Outputs: the advected data VDBs and the velocity VDB"]

- Caveats: ["Requires DOP-level permissions; not in Houdini Core, only in FX, Apprentice, Indie and Education.", "The page covers the base node available since 21.0 and does not describe a 2.0 variant.", "Search snippets listed different Trace Method options than the fetched page; the fetched page text is used here."]

